  "body": "### What happens\nThe material-sim gouge check sweeps a **tap as a solid cylinder of its full (major) diameter**, the same as a drill or an end mill (`crates/app/src/stock_sim.rs` ~L240: \"everything whose cut IS its cylinder - end mills, drills, taps, bores - sweeps the disc\").\n\nA tapped hole is normally modelled at the **tap-drill (minor) size**, with the thread shown cosmetically or not at all. On such a model every tap operation is refused at post time.\n\n### Repro (found testing PR #259)\n- Model: a plate with one blind Ø4 mm hole (also tried with the thread modelled).\n- Drilling → Drill, tool `TAP-M6` (Ø6, 1.0 pitch), Source = the model, Detect holes → 1 hole Ø0.1575\".\n- Generate: OK. Post: `post refused: post blocked - gouge: program cuts below the model in 113 cell(s), worst 3.54 mm at (-0.64, -2.89, z -1.00)`.\n- Remodel the hole at Ø6 (the tap's major diameter): the post passes.\n\nSo today the only way to post a tap is to model the hole at the thread's major diameter, which is not how CAD models of tapped holes look.\n\n### Expected\nA tap cuts only the thread band, between the hole's drilled wall and the tap's major diameter. The check should not call that a gouge when the tap rides an existing hole of at least roughly the tap-drill size. Possible shapes for the fix:\n- For a Tap tool, sweep the tap's **minor (core) diameter**, `diameter - pitch` (ISO metric approx.; e.g. M6x1 → Ø5). The thread flanks are intended removal, not a gouge.\n- And/or keep the full-diameter check only where the tap would enter solid material with no hole (a tap into an undrilled spot *is* a crash).\n\nPinning test: a tap on a hole modelled at the tap-drill size posts; the same tap where no hole exists is still refused.\n\nSuggested labels (the filer can't set labels): `bug`, `P1`, `area:sim`, `area:drilling`, `source:agent`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
